"severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: tooling.md's paths miss the new contract project's pyproject.toml", "recommendation": "Read core.md, display.md and tooling.md (the list `learnings-files --for-diff` printed). The change does not go back to any pattern a rule warns against, so no severity is escalated. One corpus gap: tooling.md's `paths:` list `pyproject.toml`, `arrt/pyproject.toml`, `arrt-player/pyproject.toml` and `tests/preferences/**`. The new `contract/pyproject.toml` carries a fourth ruff/black configuration with `select = [\"ALL\"]`, and the RUF100 autofixer rule ('Run an autofixer only under the project's real rule set') governs exactly that file, but it does not load when a session edits only `contract/`. That is a glob that misses the code it governs. Scope: instance. Add `contract/pyproject.toml` (and `contract/tests/**` if the git ls-files rule should follow) to tooling.md's `paths:`. Rules that bear on the observations in this partial: 'When a comment justifies code by a constraint' (the root pyproject's jsonschema/referencing comment) and 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo' (the surviving 'root suite' wording in player-contract.md).", "files": [".claude/rules/learnings/tooling.md", "contract/pyproject.toml"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: no open item resolved by this change", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `cache-query affecting` over the 43 changed paths (store age about 85 min, no sync_error) returned #119, #120, #145, #166 and #263. None is resolved: they matched only on CLAUDE.md, arrt/tests/unit, pyproject.toml and test_file_organization.py, and the diff does not touch their substance. Searches for semantic_errors, arrt-contract, 'contract package' and 'pin the contract' found no item about this work. C-B3: the diff touches area `tests`, which has open items, and no chunk updated them. Two of them bear on this change. #166: the root's Python 3.12 parses arrt's 3.14 source, and the contract package adds a fourth project with its own `requires-python >=3.12` and no pinned interpreter. #263: git ls-files guards skip untracked files, and `test_file_organization._TEST_TREES` now includes `contract/tests/`. Assess both and update their status.", "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 2}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The installed-wheel branch of root() and specification() is never executed by any test", "recommendation": "Rated WARNING (boundary rating). A Player outside this repository relies on exactly one code path: root() and specification() resolving `_HERE` (data inside the package). Every suite runs against the editable install, so all of them resolve the second candidate, `_HERE.parents[1]`. test_the_wheel_carries_the_data_inside_the_package only lists the zip's names, and test_a_package_without_its_data_refuses_by_name only covers refusal. If `_HERE` were dropped from either candidate tuple, or the two were reordered, every suite would stay green. Done-when 4 says the wheel 'reads cleanly in an isolated interpreter', but no test checks that claim. Core rule: 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value'. Fix for this instance: in the wheel test, extract the built wheel into tmp_path/site. Put it first on sys.path, using the same sys.modules swap the refusal test uses, and assert the following: Path(bare.root()) is inside tmp_path; bare.specification() resolves inside the package; bare.validator('schemas/manifest.v2.schema.json') builds; and bare.fixtures() is non-empty. specification()'s refusal path (no player-contract.md in either place) also has no test, so assert it in the existing refusal test. Scope: instance.", "files": ["contract/tests/test_package.py", "contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "record_lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 01 cites tests/preferences/test_player_contract.py, which this chunk moved", "recommendation": "record_lint reports `chunk-ref-missing` at build-plan-wave-5-split.md line 177: the declared deliverable `tests/preferences/test_player_contract.py` does not exist. The work was not dropped. That line names the file as the source the functions are 'lifted from', and the chunk moved it whole to contract/tests/test_schemas_and_fixtures.py, with semantic_errors, schema, registry and validator now in arrt_contract. Of the three moved files, I checked every assertion against the HEAD versions: none was dropped, and the compound `and` asserts in test_vectors.py were split one-for-one. Reword the plan line to name the file as the former location, or name its new home, so the check stops firing on every later round. The accompanying `unchecked` entry ('graded chunk 01 ... inferred') is a NOTE: supply the chunk explicitly at the next critic-begin. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-5-split.md"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Consumers bypass the package's validator()/registry()/routes() and rebuild them by hand, including a second schema registry", "recommendation": "Class. Chunk 01 says the plan 'lifted' schema(), the registry and validator() into arrt_contract, and player-contract.md \u00a7 The package says 'validator(path) registers every schema, so a reference between schemas resolves'. Even so, almost every plane reader still calls root() and then builds its own Draft202012Validator from the raw JSON. The clearest case is arrt/tests/contract/test_label_surface.py:27-34, which still assembles its own referencing.Registry for the label schema's cross-$ref to manifest.v2. That is the exact logic registry() now owns, and it is the one import of `referencing` in a plane whose pyproject does not declare it. The other hand-built sites drift on format_checker. Sites without one: test_client_surface.py:29, test_label_surface.py:36 (_CLIENT) and server_double.py:130. Sites with one: arrt-player test_player_contract.py:170 and :307, test_display_state.py:28 and test_screen.py:212. server_double.py:32 also re-parses routes.json even though routes() exists, and so do arrt/tests/contract/test_client_surface.py:23 and test_label_surface.py:23. The consequence: if a second schema gains a cross-schema $ref, every hand-built validator for it fails to resolve, and each one has to be found again. The package that exists to prevent this does not. Remedy, a construction rather than a list: send each validator through arrt_contract.validator(path) and each route read through routes(). To find the members, grep the three suites for `Draft202012Validator(` and `\"routes.json\"`, excluding check_schema. Then the plane's direct `referencing` import goes away instead of needing a declaration.", "files": ["arrt/tests/contract/test_label_surface.py", "arrt/tests/contract/test_client_surface.py", "arrt-player/tests/server_double.py", "arrt-player/tests/test_player_contract.py", "arrt-player/tests/test_display_state.py", "arrt-player/tests/test_screen.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Root pyproject still declares jsonschema and referencing for a 'root contract test' that moved into contract/tests", "recommendation": "Instance. The root dev group keeps `jsonschema>=4.23` ('The Player contract's schemas are checked with this') and `referencing>=0.35` ('Imported directly by the root contract test to resolve one schema's $ref'). Both reasons left with the git mv of tests/preferences/test_player_contract.py into contract/tests/. A search for `^(import|from) (jsonschema|referencing)` outside arrt/, arrt-player/ and contract/ now finds nothing. arrt-contract already brings both in as runtime dependencies. Delete the two declarations and their comments, or, if a root test still needs one, point the comment at that test. As things stand, a