 both entries, since `arrt-contract` brings them transitively and nothing at the root imports them. Also check the root's other declared deps against what the root still imports after 02.", "files": ["pyproject.toml"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "semantic_errors is now a published API and raises on a schema-invalid document instead of reporting", "recommendation": "`arrt_contract.semantic_errors` indexes `document[\"works\"]`, `document[\"schedule\"][\"slots\"]` and the horizon directly, and passes every instant to `datetime.fromisoformat`. On a document that fails the schema (no `works`, no `schedule`, a malformed instant) it raises KeyError, TypeError or ValueError, and it does not return a list. `arrt/tests/feed_guard.problems` calls it unconditionally after the schema errors, so a builder regression that drops `works` surfaces as a traceback, and the schema messages are lost with it. The behaviour came over unchanged from the root test. What changed is that a Player outside this repo now consumes it as the reference statement, and its docstring states no precondition. Scope: class (the package's public functions that take an unvalidated document). Either state the precondition in the docstring ('call on a document the schema accepts') and have `problems()` skip semantics when schema errors exist, or make the function total over malformed input.", "files": ["contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py", "arrt/tests/feed_guard.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Records Pass: player-contract.md, now shipped in the wheel, still credits the moved checks to 'the root suite'", "recommendation": "Clears the 'it ships' bar. `specification()` and the wheel's force-include now publish `.prawduct/artifacts/player-contract.md` to every Player. Two sentences still say 'the root suite'. One is \u00a7 Major 2 settings (`facts`: 'a copy the root suite holds to the label definition'); that test is now `contract/tests/test_schemas_and_fixtures.py` and the sentence gives no path. The other is \u00a7 Time ('The root suite holds them to a reference statement'), next to the corrected `contract/tests/test_vectors.py` path. A Player maintainer reading the released spec is sent to a suite they do not have, and after 05 Arrt's root suite will not hold it either. ('When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording'.) Scope: instance, bounded by grepping the artifacts for 'root suite' next to contract nouns. Make both sentences relational ('the package's own suite') or delete the attribution.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/player-contract.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "A MED-impact inference (git tag rather than PyPI) is now written as the release rule in the shipped specification", "recommendation": "The plan's Open assumptions mark 'published means an annotated tag contract-v<version>, installed by git URL' as *Inferred*, [MED impact | user can override], with a builder's `[DECISION ... user can veto]`. `player-contract.md` \u00a7 The package now states the tag form, and the tag-is-not-moved/uv.lock reasoning, as the contract's release rule, and that text ships in the wheel. The veto is recorded, so this is not a norm born without a decision. But the owner's answer to Goal 2 was 'a published package', and the inference about what 'published' means has not been confirmed by the owner. Confirm it with the owner before 03 pushes `contract-v1.0.0`, because a tag pushed is the one step a later PyPI ruling cannot simply swap out.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/player-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-wave-5-split.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Mid-build decisions for chunk 01, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Four decisions were recorded. (1) The data stays at `contract/{schemas,fixtures,vectors,routes.json}` and the